The 3-clause BSD license above covers ijkpp as a whole, EXCEPT for the files
listed below.

Third-party and derivative components
=====================================

1. media/filters/legacy/ — GNU Lesser General Public License v2.1 or later
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------
   The files in this directory are line-by-line ports of algorithms from
   ijkplayer's ff_ffplay.c, and are therefore derivative works of it:

     legacy/video_frame_compositor.{h,cc}   port of video_refresh() and
                                            compute_target_delay()
     legacy/av_sync_controller.{h,cc}       port of get_master_sync_type(),
                                            get_master_clock(),
                                            synchronize_audio() and
                                            synchronize_audio_to_video()
     legacy/clock.{h,cc}                    port of struct Clock
                                            (get_clock/set_clock/set_clock_at)

   Copyright (c) 2013-2026 Zhang Rui <bbcallen@gmail.com>
   Copyright (c) 2013-2026 Bilibili
   Copyright (c) 2003-2013 Fabrice Bellard (ffplay.c)
   Copyright (c) 2026 The ijkpp Authors (C++20 rewrite, structure and tests)

   Licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License version 2.1 or later.
   The full license text is in media/filters/legacy/LICENSE.LGPL-2.1.

   This is decision D10 / risk R8 in docs/08 §4 and §5. The quarantine into a
   single directory is deliberate: a downstream integrator can establish the
   license boundary of the whole project by listing one directory instead of
   reading every file header. Consequences for integrators:

     * Linking against libijkpp.so gives you the LGPL's relinking rights over
       these objects; replacing them requires rebuilding the shared library.
     * A static-link build must be accompanied by the object files or the
       complete corresponding source code, per LGPL-2.1 §6.
     * Do not add non-ported code to media/filters/legacy/, and do not move
       these files out of it, without a legal review.

2. base/ — mirrored API surface from Chromium's base/ (BSD-3-Clause)
   ------------------------------------------------------------------
   Files under base/ re-implement, with the same names and the same semantics,
   a minimal subset of Chromium's base/ library (Copyright (c) the Chromium
   Authors, BSD-3-Clause). Each such file names its Chromium counterpart in a
   header comment. The implementation is original to ijkpp; no Chromium source
   is vendored. The BSD-3-Clause license above is chosen for compatibility.

3. platform/ffmpeg/ and media/filters/ffmpeg_*.{h,cc} — FFmpeg adaptation
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   These files are original ijkpp code (BSD-3-Clause) but they are the only
   place in the project that includes libav*/libsw* headers and the only CMake
   target that links FFmpeg (decision D11). FFmpeg itself is not redistributed
   here; ijkpp builds against the system FFmpeg (LGPL-2.1+ or GPL-2+,
   depending on how your distribution configured it). Your resulting binary's
   license is therefore also bounded by the FFmpeg build you link against.

4. Tests and tools
   ----------------
   tests/testdata/*.mp4 and *.m4a are synthetic media generated with FFmpeg's
   lavfi sources (sine, testsrc2); they carry no third-party copyright.
   GoogleTest is detected via find_package and is not vendored.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

NOTE FOR MAINTAINERS: the "Copyright (c) 2026, The ijkpp Authors" line above
is a placeholder until the contributor list or a legal entity is settled
(open question Q1 in docs/08 §7). docs/08 §5 R8 records that a legal review is
still outstanding; if the project must ship as pure BSD-3-Clause, the ported
algorithms in media/filters/legacy/ have to be re-derived independently, which
docs/08 estimates at +2 weeks.
